From bdb0dd20244a5413b1056b6ca3c6173048484bd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 21:28:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] upnp: restore mappings after a firewall rebuild, IGD:2 by
 default, protect the  router's own ports

Evaluated whether a newer or different UPnP package would help: it would not.
The tree already carries miniupnpd 2.3.10 plus post-release git, and 2.3.10 is
still upstream's newest tagged release. UPnP IGD:2 (2010) remains the latest
gateway specification - there is no IGD:3 - and the genuinely modern protocol,
PCP (RFC 6887), is already implemented and enabled here. The breakage was all
in the wiring around the package.

1. Every firewall rebuild silently killed all live mappings. firewall.c applies
   its rulesets with iptables-restore WITHOUT --noflush, and the payload
   declares :VUPNP, :PUPNP and :FUPNP - so each rebuild flushes every UPnP,
   NAT-PMP and PCP mapping. The only recovery hook, reload_upnp(), sent
   SIGUSR1, whose handler merely sets should_send_public_address_change_notif;
   it re-adds nothing. miniupnpd kept the mappings in memory and in
   /tmp/upnp.leases and kept answering clients "that mapping already exists",
   so nothing retried, while no packet was forwarded. Silent, non-self-healing,
   and reported as healthy by every layer.
   reload_upnp() now performs a real restart via notify_rc("restart_upnp").
   miniupnpd writes its lease file on clean shutdown and re-adds every mapping
   at startup with its remaining lifetime, so a stop/start restores exactly
   what the flush destroyed. Asynchronous, because start_upnp() can block for
   5 s on the GETREALIP probe and start_firewall() must not stall on it.

2. pidof()/killall() are exact process-name matches, but the IGDv2 build runs
   as miniupnpd-igdv2. stop_upnp() killed both names; reload_upnp() and two
   NAS paths asked only for the plain one. New pidof_upnp() checks both.

3. --igd2 went only to the miniupnpd-igdv2 build, so leaving IPv6 pinholes off
   - the default, and the sensible setting here - silently dropped IPv4 clients
   to IGD:1, costing AddAnyPortMapping ("that port is taken, give me any free
   one", the two-consoles collision, made likelier because CHECK_PORTINUSE is
   on) and GetListOfPortMappings. The default daemon is now built --igd2 too.
   Verified by preprocessor probe that --igd2 without --ipv6 leaves only IGD_V2
   and ENABLE_PCP defined: ENABLE_6FC_SERVICE and ENABLE_UPNPPINHOLE are nested
   inside both #ifdef IGD_V2 and #ifdef ENABLE_IPV6, so every pinhole path is
   compiled out. Backward compatible - UPNP_STRICT is unset, so minissdp.c
   matches M-SEARCH by prefix and answers IGD:1 searches with an IGD:1 ST.

4. Hardening. The shipped default emitted "allow 1-65535", making the else
   branch that promises "only ports above 1024" unreachable, and the deny list
   omitted the router's own OpenVPN, WireGuard, IPSec and SSH listeners while
   the plain-HTTP admin deny sat behind an #if 0 that could not have compiled.
   Since VUPNP hooks nat/PREROUTING, ahead of the routing decision, a LAN host
   could map ext:51820 to itself and intercept inbound WireGuard; secure_mode
   does not help, because mapping the VPN port to yourself is the attack.
   upnp_protect_svc (default on) emits denies for those ports regardless of the
   allowed range, so it also protects boxes still set to 1.

The pinhole toggle is relabelled - it used to read "Enable IGDv2 (IPv6 pinhole
support)" while actually deciding both things - and "Protect router service
ports" is exposed next to it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/Makefile                   |  13 +-
 release/src/router/rc/rc.h                    |   1 +
 release/src/router/rc/services.c              | 123 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../src/router/www/Advanced_WAN_Content.asp   |  28 +++-
 4 files changed, 153 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/Makefile b/release/src/router/Makefile
index 1604f5cafa..e0784d6327 100644
--- a/release/src/router/Makefile
+++ b/release/src/router/Makefile
@@ -4584,7 +4584,18 @@ miniupnpd/config.h: shared/version.h
 ifeq ($(RTCONFIG_IGD2),y)
 	cd miniupnpd-igdv2 && ./configure --vendorcfg --leasefile $(if $(RTCONFIG_IPV6),--ipv6,) --igd2 --portinuse $(if $(RTCONFIG_AURASYNC),--aurasync,) $(if $(RTCONFIG_GEFORCENOW),--nvgfn) --iptablespath=$(TOP)/$(IPTABLES)
 endif
-	cd miniupnpd && ./configure --vendorcfg --leasefile --portinuse $(if $(RTCONFIG_AURASYNC),--aurasync,) $(if $(RTCONFIG_GEFORCENOW),--nvgfn) --iptablespath=$(TOP)/$(IPTABLES)
+	# reaper 2026-08-11: build the default daemon as an IGD:2 device too. IGD:2 and IPv6
+	# pinholes are separable - in the generated config.h, ENABLE_6FC_SERVICE (the IPv6
+	# firewall service) is nested inside BOTH "#ifdef IGD_V2" and "#ifdef ENABLE_IPV6",
+	# and ENABLE_UPNPPINHOLE needs one of those - so --igd2 WITHOUT --ipv6 gives IGD:2
+	# with every pinhole path compiled out. Previously --igd2 went only to the
+	# miniupnpd-igdv2 build, so leaving IPv6 pinholes off (the default, and the sensible
+	# setting for a hardened build) silently also dropped IPv4 clients to IGD:1 and cost
+	# AddAnyPortMapping ("that port is taken, give me any free one" - the two-consoles /
+	# two-torrent-clients collision) and GetListOfPortMappings. Backward compatibility is
+	# safe: UPNP_STRICT is not set, so minissdp.c matches an M-SEARCH ST by prefix only
+	# and answers IGD:1 searches with an IGD:1 ST, as UDA v1.1 requires.
+	cd miniupnpd && ./configure --vendorcfg --leasefile --igd2 --portinuse $(if $(RTCONFIG_AURASYNC),--aurasync,) $(if $(RTCONFIG_GEFORCENOW),--nvgfn) --iptablespath=$(TOP)/$(IPTABLES)
 
 miniupnpd: $(IPTABLES) e2fsprogs miniupnpd/config.h
 	@$(SEP)
diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/rc.h b/release/src/router/rc/rc.h
index f84f3db0e3..3219860a5b 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/rc.h
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/rc.h
@@ -2981,6 +2981,7 @@ extern void start_rchqd(void);
 extern void stop_gk(void);	/* reaper v1.6.8 Gatekeeper device access control (rc/gatekeeper.c) */
 extern void start_gk(void);
 extern void gk_teardown_rules(void);
+extern int pidof_upnp(void);	/* reaper: pid of miniupnpd OR miniupnpd-igdv2 (exact-name pidof misses the latter) */
 extern void stop_rwarden(void);	/* reaper v1.8.0 Warden ipset threat-feed/geo/manual-IP firewall (rc/rwarden.c) */
 extern void start_rwarden(void);
 extern void rw_teardown_rules(void);
diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/services.c b/release/src/router/rc/services.c
index 9109d36897..d80cecd9a3 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/services.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/services.c
@@ -8018,21 +8018,70 @@ void start_upnp(void)
 				}
 
 				if (nvram_get_int("misc_http_x")) {
+					int enable = 0;
 #ifdef RTCONFIG_HTTPS
-					int enable = nvram_get_int("http_enable");
+					enable = nvram_get_int("http_enable");
 					if (enable != 0) {
 						httpx_port = nvram_get_int("misc_httpsport_x") ? : 8443;
 						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n", httpx_port);
 					}
 #endif
-#if 0
-					if (enable != 1)
-
-					{
+					/* reaper 2026-08-11: the plain-HTTP admin port was left behind an
+					   #if 0 - `enable` was declared inside the RTCONFIG_HTTPS block, so
+					   the dead branch could not have compiled even if re-enabled. With
+					   the declaration hoisted it works. http_enable: 0 = HTTP only,
+					   1 = HTTPS only, 2 = both -> deny the HTTP port whenever the web
+					   UI answers HTTP at all. Without this, remote web admin on 8080
+					   was UPnP-hijackable while the HTTPS port was protected. */
+					if (enable != 1) {
 						httpx_port = nvram_get_int("misc_httpport_x") ? : 8080;
 						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n", httpx_port);
 					}
-#endif
+				}
+
+				/* reaper 2026-08-11 (UPnP hardening): never hand out a mapping on a
+				 * port THIS ROUTER listens on for inbound WAN traffic. VUPNP hooks
+				 * nat/PREROUTING, which is evaluated before the routing decision, so a
+				 * UPnP/NAT-PMP/PCP mapping on e.g. the WireGuard port silently steals
+				 * inbound VPN traffic from the router. secure_mode=yes does not help:
+				 * it only restricts a client to mapping to ITSELF, and mapping the VPN
+				 * port to itself is precisely the attack. The stock deny list covered
+				 * virtual-server entries, the HTTPS admin port, WebDAV, AiCloud and
+				 * DownloadMaster - but none of the VPN or SSH listeners.
+				 * These denies are emitted regardless of the allowed external range,
+				 * so they also protect boxes whose upnp_min_port_ext is still 1. */
+				if (!nvram_match("upnp_protect_svc", "0")) {
+					char svc[64], *sp, *ssave = NULL;
+
+					if (nvram_get_int("wgs_enable"))
+						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n",
+							nvram_get_int("wgs_port") ? : 51820);
+
+					/* vpn_serverx_start is the list of RUNNING OpenVPN server units;
+					   the canonical reader is foreach_44() (rc/dnsfilter.c:194), i.e.
+					   comma-separated - whitespace is tolerated here as well */
+					strlcpy(svc, nvram_safe_get("vpn_serverx_start"), sizeof(svc));
+					for (sp = strtok_r(svc, ", ", &ssave); sp; sp = strtok_r(NULL, ", ", &ssave)) {
+						char key[24];
+						int u = atoi(sp);
+						if (u < 1 || u > 2)
+							continue;
+						snprintf(key, sizeof(key), "vpn_server%d_port", u);
+						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n",
+							nvram_get_int(key) ? : 1194);
+					}
+
+					if (nvram_get_int("ipsec_server_enable")) {
+						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n",
+							nvram_get_int("ipsec_isakmp_port") ? : 500);
+						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n",
+							nvram_get_int("ipsec_nat_t_port") ? : 4500);
+					}
+
+					/* sshd_enable: 1 = WAN+LAN, 2 = LAN only -> only 1 is exposed */
+					if (nvram_get_int("sshd_enable") == 1)
+						fprintf(f, "deny %d 0.0.0.0/0 0-65535\n",
+							nvram_get_int("sshd_port") ? : 22);
 				}
 
 #ifdef RTCONFIG_WEBDAV
@@ -8150,6 +8199,23 @@ void start_upnp(void)
 	}
 }
 
+/* reaper 2026-08-11: the IGDv2 build runs under a DIFFERENT process name
+ * ("miniupnpd-igdv2", see the xstart() above), and pidof()/killall() are exact
+ * strcmp() matches on the process name (shared/process.c:71). So a bare
+ * pidof("miniupnpd") silently misses the daemon whenever upnp_pinhole_enable is
+ * set. stop_upnp() below already kills both names - proof the two names were
+ * known - but the three other call sites only ever asked about the plain one.
+ * Ask about both, everywhere. */
+int pidof_upnp(void)
+{
+	int pid = pidof("miniupnpd");
+#ifdef RTCONFIG_IGD2
+	if (pid < 0)
+		pid = pidof("miniupnpd-igdv2");
+#endif
+	return pid;
+}
+
 void stop_upnp(void)
 {
 	if (getpid() != 1 && getuid() != 0) {
@@ -8167,6 +8233,37 @@ void stop_upnp(void)
 #endif
 }
 
+/* Called at the very end of start_firewall().
+ *
+ * reaper 2026-08-11 field fix ("UPnP is broken / port forwards stop working
+ * after a while"). The firewall rebuild applies its rulesets with
+ * iptables-restore WITHOUT --noflush, and those payloads declare
+ * ":VUPNP - [0:0]", ":PUPNP - [0:0]" and ":FUPNP - [0:0]" - so EVERY rebuild
+ * flushes EVERY live UPnP / NAT-PMP / PCP mapping. There are ~29 restart_firewall
+ * call sites across rc and httpd (WAN reconnect, DDNS, VPN toggles, saving any
+ * firewall or port-forward setting, NAS app changes, watchdog recovery), plus
+ * Reaper's own Warden and Gatekeeper arming.
+ *
+ * This used to send SIGUSR1, which re-adds NOTHING: miniupnpd's SIGUSR1 handler
+ * (miniupnpd.c:816) only sets should_send_public_address_change_notif, i.e.
+ * "announce a new WAN IP". So the daemon kept every mapping in its in-memory
+ * list and in /tmp/upnp.leases, kept answering clients "that mapping already
+ * exists" (so the client never retried), and the GUI kept listing them - while
+ * not one packet was forwarded. clean_ruleset_interval only ever REMOVES rules,
+ * so nothing ever recovered. Silent, non-self-healing, and reported as healthy
+ * by every layer: exactly why this reads as "the package is broken".
+ *
+ * Fix: actually restart the daemon. miniupnpd writes its lease file on clean
+ * shutdown (lease_file_rewrite(), miniupnpd.c:3381) and reload_from_lease_file()
+ * re-adds every mapping at startup (upnpredirect.c:155, called from
+ * miniupnpd.c:2265) - with the REMAINING lifetime, because Merlin compiles in
+ * LEASEFILE_USE_REMAINING_TIME. A stop/start therefore restores precisely what
+ * the flush destroyed. This is also what OpenWrt does on a firewall reload.
+ *
+ * Routed through notify_rc() so it is ASYNCHRONOUS: start_upnp() can block for
+ * up to 5 s on the RTCONFIG_GETREALIP probe below, and start_firewall() must not
+ * stall on that. No debounce: every completed rebuild genuinely needs a reinstall,
+ * and a burst during WAN-up simply ends with the last restart winning. */
 void reload_upnp(void)
 {
 	char tmp[100], prefix[sizeof("wanXXXXXXXXXX_")];
@@ -8182,8 +8279,14 @@ void reload_upnp(void)
 #endif
 	snprintf(prefix, sizeof(prefix), "wan%d_", unit);
 
-	if (nvram_get_int(strcat_r(prefix, "upnp_enable", tmp)))
-		killall("miniupnpd", SIGUSR1);
+	if (!nvram_get_int(strcat_r(prefix, "upnp_enable", tmp)))
+		return;
+	/* not running -> the normal start path owns it; there is nothing to restore
+	   and we must not spawn a daemon the user did not ask for */
+	if (pidof_upnp() < 0)
+		return;
+
+	notify_rc("restart_upnp");
 }
 
 int
@@ -18661,7 +18764,7 @@ check_ddr_done:
 		}
 		if(action&RC_SERVICE_START){
 			int restart_upnp = 0;
-			if (pidof("miniupnpd") != -1) {
+			if (pidof_upnp() != -1) {	/* reaper: also matches miniupnpd-igdv2 */
 				stop_upnp();
 				restart_upnp = 1;
 			}
@@ -23782,7 +23885,7 @@ int run_app_script(const char *pkg_name, const char *pkg_action)
 	if(pkg_action == NULL || strlen(pkg_action) <= 0)
 		return -1;
 
-	if (pidof("miniupnpd") != -1) {
+	if (pidof_upnp() != -1) {	/* reaper: also matches miniupnpd-igdv2 */
 		stop_upnp();
 		restart_upnp = 1;
 	}
diff --git a/release/src/router/www/Advanced_WAN_Content.asp b/release/src/router/www/Advanced_WAN_Content.asp
index 265a39171e..7d9082432d 100644
--- a/release/src/router/www/Advanced_WAN_Content.asp
+++ b/release/src/router/www/Advanced_WAN_Content.asp
@@ -493,6 +493,8 @@ function display_upnp_options(){
 	document.getElementById("upnp_range_int").style.display = (document.form.wan_upnp_enable[0].checked) ? "" : "none";
 	document.getElementById("upnp_range_ext").style.display = (document.form.wan_upnp_enable[0].checked) ? "" : "none";
 	document.getElementById("upnp_secure_tr").style.display = (document.form.wan_upnp_enable[0].checked) ? "" : "none";
+	/* reaper 2026-08-11: router-service-port protection follows the master UPnP switch */
+	document.getElementById("upnp_protect_svc_tr").style.display = (document.form.wan_upnp_enable[0].checked) ? "" : "none";
 	if (igd2_support) {
 		document.getElementById("upnp_pinhole").style.display = (document.form.wan_upnp_enable[0].checked) ? "" : "none";
 	} else {
@@ -2591,11 +2593,21 @@ function get_default_wan_name(){
 									<input type="radio" name="wan_upnp_enable" class="input" value="0" onclick="display_upnp_options();return change_common_radio(this, 'LANHostConfig', 'wan_upnp_enable', '0')" <% nvram_match("wan_upnp_enable", "0", "checked"); %>><#checkbox_No#>
 								</td>
 							</tr>
+							<!-- reaper 2026-08-11: this row used to read "Enable IGDv2 (IPv6 pinhole
+							     support)" and it bundled two unrelated things - it selected the
+							     miniupnpd-igdv2 binary, so turning it OFF also silently dropped the
+							     router to IGD:1 on IPv4 and gave up AddAnyPortMapping (the "port
+							     already taken, give me any free one" action that stops two consoles
+							     or two torrent clients from colliding) and GetListOfPortMappings.
+							     The plain daemon is now built with --igd2 as well, so IGD:2 is always
+							     on and this switch controls only what its name says: the IPv6
+							     firewall-pinhole service (WANIPv6FirewallControl). -->
 							<tr id="upnp_pinhole">
-								<th><a class="hintstyle" href="javascript:void(0);" onClick="openHint(50,5);">Enable IGDv2 (IPv6 pinhole support)</a></th>
+								<th><a class="hintstyle" href="javascript:void(0);" onClick="openHint(50,5);">UPnP: Allow IPv6 pinholes (IGD:2 firewall control)</a></th>
 								<td>
 									<input type="radio" name="upnp_pinhole_enable" class="input" value="1" onclick="display_upnp_options();" <% nvram_match("upnp_pinhole_enable", "1", "checked"); %>><#checkbox_Yes#>
 									<input type="radio" name="upnp_pinhole_enable" class="input" value="0" onclick="display_upnp_options();" <% nvram_match("upnp_pinhole_enable", "0", "checked"); %>><#checkbox_No#>
+									<div style="color:#FFCC00;font-size:11px;margin-top:3px;">Lets IPv6 clients open a firewall hole straight to themselves. IPv4 port mapping and PCP are unaffected by this setting.</div>
 								</td>
 							</tr>
 							<tr id="upnp_secure_tr">
@@ -2605,6 +2617,20 @@ function get_default_wan_name(){
 									<input type="radio" name="upnp_secure" class="input" value="0" <% nvram_match_x("", "upnp_secure", "0", "checked"); %>><#checkbox_No#>
 								</td>
 							</tr>
+							<!-- reaper 2026-08-11: VUPNP hooks nat/PREROUTING, which is evaluated
+							     before the routing decision, so a UPnP/PCP mapping on one of the
+							     router's OWN inbound ports takes that traffic away from the router
+							     entirely. secure_mode does not help - mapping the VPN port to
+							     yourself IS the attack. Default on; exposed so it can be turned off
+							     if someone deliberately wants a LAN host to take one of these over. -->
+							<tr id="upnp_protect_svc_tr">
+								<th>UPnP: Protect router service ports</th>
+								<td>
+									<input type="radio" name="upnp_protect_svc" class="input" value="1" <% nvram_match_x("", "upnp_protect_svc", "1", "checked"); %>><#checkbox_Yes#>
+									<input type="radio" name="upnp_protect_svc" class="input" value="0" <% nvram_match_x("", "upnp_protect_svc", "0", "checked"); %>><#checkbox_No#>
+									<div style="color:#FFCC00;font-size:11px;margin-top:3px;">Refuses UPnP/PCP requests for the ports this router listens on itself &mdash; OpenVPN, WireGuard, IPSec, SSH and web admin &mdash; so a LAN device cannot intercept inbound traffic meant for the router.</div>
+								</td>
+							</tr>
 							<tr id="upnp_range_int">
 								<th>UPNP: Allowed internal port range</th>
 									<td>
